# Temporal scope and spoilers

Before researching or editing historical material:

1. Read `docs/temporal-scope.md`.
2. Select the contract in `docs/content-contracts.md`.
3. Declare event time, public-knowledge time, `knowledge_cutoff`, and categorical
   `spoiler_scope` in the document's sibling `.meta.yaml`.
4. Use no knowledge beyond that boundary in claims, analysis, or reader-facing
   prose. Published prose must never announce the boundary, spoilers, or that a
   claim is “only an expectation / not the outcome”; write as a journalist in
   that moment (`docs/methodology.md`).

Treat direct future outcomes, accidents, hindsight, foreshadowing,
outcome-shaped emphasis, and retrospective certainty as spoilers. Do not treat
an unknown magazine day-of-month as an automatic quarantine when the cited
passage is clearly pre-cutoff; follow content-based availability and spoiler
calibration in `docs/source-policy.md` and `docs/temporal-scope.md`. A later
source does not widen scope. A research record may identify and quarantine a
spoiler-bearing source under `docs/source-policy.md`; that recordkeeping does
not make later knowledge usable. Remove vague dramatic outcome signals as well
as explicit results. Remove published anti-spoiler meta without widening scope.

This rule is intended for the provisional future `archive/` and `research/`
roots; apply it manually to any historical draft stored elsewhere.
